            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Geometric Twins"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.556,
            0.108
          ],
          "content": "3. In the figure below, $AB = AD, CB = CD.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.113,
            0.847,
            0.153
          ],
          "content": "Can you identify any pair of congruent triangles? If yes, explain why they are congruent."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.157,
            0.838,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "Does AC divide $\\angle  BAD$ and $\\angle  BCD$ into two equal parts? Give reasons."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.399,
            0.188,
            0.6,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.442,
            0.848,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": "4. In the figure below, are ΔDFE and ΔGED congruent to each other? It is given that $\\mathrm { D F } = \\mathrm { D G }$ and $\\mathrm{FE} \\equiv \\mathrm{GE}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.313,
            0.486,
            0.63,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.713,
            0.423,
            0.736
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Measuring the Angles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.74,
            0.849,
            0.799
          ],
          "content": "Instead of measuring the three sidelengths of the triangular frame, if Meera and Rabia measure the three angles, can they recreate the triangle exactly?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.807,
            0.848,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": "？ Suppose the angles are 30°, 70°, and $8 0 ^ { \\circ } .$ Can we create an exact copy of the frame with this?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.852,
            0.848,
            0.892
          ],
          "content": "As we see, we can draw many triangles with these measurements that are not congruent."
        },
        {
